    fix: report a short write, and stop trusting an ESC-only assertion

    Copilot's three findings on the escaping guard.

    Writer.Write discarded the sink's byte count, so a sink that wrote part of
    the sanitized buffer and reported no error was reported to the caller as a
    full successful write. That is a contract violation by the sink, not a state
    this writer can reach on its own — but truncation is exactly how a
    half-written escape stops being an escape, and silence about it is worse
    than the violation. It returns io.ErrShortWrite now.

    The Line doc referred to a String function this package does not have; the
    shared behaviour lives in Writer and Bytes. Two stale references in the test
    file said the same thing.

    TestLineEscapesLayoutBytes scanned Line's output for ESC and a DECODED
    U+009B, which cannot see a raw 0x9b byte at all — the exact half of the rule
    that two review rounds have now had to add. It uses a decoding scan that
    catches a control in every spelling: ESC, a decoded C1 code point, and an
    undecodable byte in the C1 range. Decoding rather than scanning bytes is
    what keeps it off the continuation bytes of ordinary runes, U+0800 being
    0xe0 0xa0 0x80. With the stray-C1 rule removed it now names five cases the
    old assertion let through.

